WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.668
[music playing]

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:03.803
 My name is Justin Workman.

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.204
I'm 39.

00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:07.606 line:0%
And I've been bladesmithing
for roughly four years.

00:00:07.673 --> 00:00:09.542
The last three months, I've
been going through chemo

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.644
with the hopes that it
would stave off the need

00:00:11.744 --> 00:00:13.513
for amputation of my right arm.

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:17.216
I hope this sets an example
to anybody that's struggling.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:22.422
No matter how bad it is, you
can still power through it.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:23.389
 My name is Doug Zegel.

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:24.891
I'm from Mount
Laurel, New Jersey.

00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:26.926 line:7%
I'm 33 years old.

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:28.494
My uncle was a blacksmith.

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:31.597
So I got to move some metal
with him when I was younger.

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:33.233
And I really enjoyed it.

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:35.868
You took something rock hard,
like steel, you heat it up,

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.336
and it's like Play-Doh.

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:40.139
I mean, what's cooler than that?

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:41.507
GRADY POWELL: Justin,
Doug, congratulations.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:43.476
You guys made it past
round one and two.

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.812
And now it's just
between the two of you

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:47.146
for the title of "Forged
in Fire" Champion

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:48.848
and a check for $10,000.

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:50.416
In round three of
the competition,

00:00:50.482 --> 00:00:52.084
we're going to send you home
to make another culinary tool.

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:53.553
But this one is
quite a bit bigger

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:54.721
than anything you've seen.

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:59.159
And that tool is
the maguro bocho.

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.062
NARRATOR: Also known
as the tuna sword,

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.797
the maguro bocho
is a specialized

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:07.700
cutting tool designed to
fillet massive bluefin tuna.

00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:10.536
This impressive blade is not
your average kitchen cutlery.

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.505
With a total length
up to three feet long,

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:14.506
these culinary swords
were often hand

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:16.609
forged by the same
swordsmiths that made

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:18.143
the iconic Japanese katana.

00:01:18.243 --> 00:01:20.146
They feature a thick
spine and razor-sharp

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:23.183
edge that cuts effortlessly
through any large fish.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:26.853
The maguro bocho is still used
today by some Japanese chefs

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.988
and can be seen on
full display at some

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:32.225
of Tokyo's iconic fish markets.

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:33.526
 Now, when you guys
build your blades,

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.228
I want you to follow
these parameters.

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:38.064
Your blade length needs to
be between 29 and 31 inches.

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:40.433
You need to have a
single-edge chisel grind.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.968
And I also want
you to incorporate

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:44.604
a ricasso between the
handle and the blade.

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:46.206
 I'm feeling pretty nervous.

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:49.208
I'm going to have to forge the
longest blade I've ever made.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.743
 Gentlemen, we'll see
you guys in four days.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:51.910
Good luck.
- All right.

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:52.779
 Good luck, man.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.020
 We're here at my home forge
in Chambersburg, Pennsylvania.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.923
My game plan is, I'm
going to san mai.

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:05.424 line:7%
Give her a little flux.

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:07.026 line:0%
JUSTIN WORKMAN (VOICEOVER):
heat treat is the biggest

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.261
thing I'm concerned with.

00:02:08.327 --> 00:02:10.530
My goal is to get
a hardened blade.

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:18.070
 Oh, I got a nice little
warp in that bad boy.

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:19.472
Oh, I'm so mad.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:20.906
JUSTIN WORKMAN (VOICEOVER):
whole competition did

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:23.042
not go exactly as I planned.

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:25.511
Got about four hours
left of this competition,

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:28.347
and I still have a fair
amount of work to do.

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.316
 I know for a fact
that I met parameters.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:32.751
It's the one thing
that I've been checking

00:02:32.851 --> 00:02:34.821
over, and over, and over again.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.059
DOUG ZEGEL: I'm back in
Mount Laurel, New Jersey,

00:02:40.125 --> 00:02:41.627
at my home forge.

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.097
And I am ready to get started
on my maguro bocho build.

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:46.932
So this tuna sword is
probably about twice as long

00:02:46.999 --> 00:02:48.768
as anything I've ever made.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:50.403
I gotta get the forge hot.

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:55.240
And I'm ready to put my 80CRV2
in there and get hammering.

00:02:55.307 --> 00:02:56.575
It's pretty malleable steel.

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:59.445
So, so far things are
going pretty quickly.

00:02:59.545 --> 00:03:02.080
But I want to keep making
progress as fast as possible.

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:03.382
Once the quench
comes, that's kind

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:05.551
of the make-or-break
situation for me today.

00:03:05.617 --> 00:03:07.420
So I'm just trying to
get the heat right.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.259
Yeah, we're in good shape.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:15.260
It's time to get everything
fit and assembled

00:03:15.327 --> 00:03:17.497
and finish this blade.

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:18.163
All right.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:19.832
Got a nice, clean fit.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:22.367
That is the first look at what
will soon be a finished blade.

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:24.403
Now that I have the
final assembly finished--

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.439
I didn't come up with
an elaborate test.

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:28.974
But I used to work in
a sushi restaurant,

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:32.011
and this is a tuna sword.

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:35.281
It cut pretty clean.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:38.017
I think it does the job.

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.418
 All right, bladesmiths.

00:03:39.518 --> 00:03:41.154
To find out what
kind of lethal damage

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:43.822
your magura bochos will
do, I'll take your weapon

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:49.161
and make some lethal cuts on
these six different fishes.

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:49.963
Justin, you're up first.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:50.896
You ready for this?

00:03:50.963 --> 00:03:53.165
 Let's make some sushi.

00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:56.703
[heavy metal music]

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.414
 Way to go, man.

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:09.315
 Thanks.

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:10.449
DOUG MARCAIDA:
All right, Justin.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:12.217
Let's talk about
your weapon here.

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:14.419
First up, your edge--

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.656
one-and-done for
all the cuts here.

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:19.125
It's a very sharp edge.

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:20.726
I like the handle
construction here.

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:24.563
It's ovoid, so my hand matches
nicely and wraps around it.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.299
Overall, sir, it will kill.

00:04:27.366 --> 00:04:29.068
 Thank you.

00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:30.570
 All right, Doug,
you're up next.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:31.169
You ready?

00:04:31.236 --> 00:04:31.937
 Have at it.

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:33.339
Let's do it.

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:37.410
[heavy metal music]

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.589
 Nicely job, man.

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:50.189
 It's all right.

00:04:50.255 --> 00:04:51.390
 All right, Doug.

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.492
First up, the handle
construction--

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.327
I really like the way you
shaped your handle here.

00:04:55.427 --> 00:04:57.163
It's typical Japanese style.

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:59.665
Now, it's a lighter blade.

00:04:59.765 --> 00:05:04.570
But your edge is so sharp, it
cut cleanly through the fishes

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:05.470
easily.

00:05:05.537 --> 00:05:08.474
Overall, sir, it will kill.

00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:08.975
 Thanks.

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:12.544
DAVID BAKER: All
right, gentlemen.

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:13.579
I think you know
what time it is.

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:15.114
It's time for the strength test.

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:18.384
And we're calling this
our bo staff attack.

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:21.421
Now, Justin, your weapon is
already locked in our device

00:05:21.521 --> 00:05:24.791
here, and I will be attacking
it with our wax wood bo staffs.

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:27.459
This test is not about what
your blades do to that staff,

00:05:27.526 --> 00:05:29.495
but what that staff's
gonna do to your blade.

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:30.896
JUSTIN WORKMAN: All right.
- Y'all ready?

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:31.864
 Let's do it.

00:05:31.931 --> 00:05:32.598
 OK.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:41.341
[heavy metal music]

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:57.022
 All right, Justin.

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:59.458
You know, this is a brutal
test for what is technically

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:01.126
a large kitchen knife.

00:06:01.226 --> 00:06:03.695
But you did very, very well.

00:06:03.762 --> 00:06:07.699
Right where my finger is,
there's the smallest of chips.

00:06:07.799 --> 00:06:09.836
And it's-- when I say
"smallest of chips,"

00:06:09.936 --> 00:06:11.670
I mean, it's really small.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.139
Everything else looks
right and tight.

00:06:14.239 --> 00:06:15.775
All in all, you did a great job.

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:16.275
You survived.

00:06:16.375 --> 00:06:17.176
 Thank you.

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:18.443
DAVID BAKER: You bet.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:19.745
All right, Doug.
You're up.

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:20.479
You ready?

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:21.680
 As ready as I can be.

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:24.450
 OK, because this
is fun for me.

00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:27.520
DOUG ZEGEL: These blades are
narrow, really thin blades.

00:06:27.586 --> 00:06:30.089
It is not designed
to take any abuse.

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:34.460
I'm already thinking that
Justin's got this test won.

00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:37.997
[heavy metal music]

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:53.378
 So Doug, right off,
this is remarkably light.

00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:55.915
There is the
slightest rough spot.

00:06:56.015 --> 00:06:59.885
But besides that, I don't see
any deformation in your blade.

00:06:59.952 --> 00:07:03.455
Blade's as straight as it
was when we started out.

00:07:03.522 --> 00:07:04.790
It's a good, solid piece.

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:05.357
Well done.

00:07:05.424 --> 00:07:06.592
 Thank you.

00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:07.726
DAVID BAKER: So all
right, gentlemen.

00:07:07.827 --> 00:07:09.796
Two tests down and
one test to go.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:14.633
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, bladesmiths,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.335
we're cutting it close.

00:07:16.402 --> 00:07:18.604
We know that both
your weapons can kill

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.072
and we know they're strong.

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.474
Now's the time to find
out how sharp they are.

00:07:22.541 --> 00:07:25.344
This is the sharpness test.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:27.313
To find out how sharp
your weapons are,

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.681
I will cut through these
rolled-up tatami mats.

00:07:29.748 --> 00:07:31.383
Now, unlike the
strength test, this

00:07:31.483 --> 00:07:33.886
is all about what the
edge of your weapon

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:35.888
will do to these mats.

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:36.556
Justin, you're up first.

00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:37.255
You ready for this?

00:07:37.356 --> 00:07:38.191
 Rock and roll.

00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:39.058
 All right, do that.

00:07:43.562 --> 00:07:46.666
[heavy metal music]

00:07:56.476 --> 00:07:58.711
All right Justin, let's
talk about the cuts here.

00:07:58.777 --> 00:08:02.348
Clearly, on the other cuts,
they cut all the way through.

00:08:02.414 --> 00:08:06.151
On the last cut, almost, but
just a little skin of that mat

00:08:06.252 --> 00:08:07.452
is hanging on.

00:08:07.519 --> 00:08:09.355
But it is a sharp edge.

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:11.757
Overall, sir, it will cut.

00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:12.691
 Thank you.

00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:13.926
 All right, Doug, your turn.

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:14.827
So you ready?

00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:15.894
 Let's see what
this thing can do.

00:08:15.995 --> 00:08:16.696
 Let's do it.

00:08:24.503 --> 00:08:27.573
[heavy metal music]

00:08:40.386 --> 00:08:41.620
All right, Doug.

00:08:41.686 --> 00:08:44.422
First cut just went
so easy without a drag

00:08:44.523 --> 00:08:46.558
that it went into
the next target.

00:08:46.625 --> 00:08:48.060
It is a very sharp weapon.

00:08:48.127 --> 00:08:49.327
It's very easy to wield.

00:08:49.428 --> 00:08:51.397
And it's light.

00:08:51.497 --> 00:08:54.566
Overall, sir, it will cut.

00:08:54.633 --> 00:08:55.834
 Thank you.

00:08:55.901 --> 00:08:57.102
GRADY POWELL: First
of all, both of you,

00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:58.436
thank you very much
for your hard work.

00:08:58.537 --> 00:08:59.939
But as we know,
only one of you is

00:09:00.006 --> 00:09:02.141
going to be leaving here the
"Forged in Fire" Champion

00:09:02.241 --> 00:09:04.042
with a check for $10,000.

00:09:04.109 --> 00:09:09.181
And today's "Forged in
Fire" champion is Doug.

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:10.482
Congratulations, you won.

00:09:10.582 --> 00:09:12.384
Justin, unfortunately,
you're not gonna

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:13.485
be leaving with the win today.

00:09:13.552 --> 00:09:14.720
And Doug is going
to tell you why.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.122
 Justin, on any other
given competition,

00:09:17.189 --> 00:09:19.257
I can see your blade
being a champion's blade.

00:09:19.324 --> 00:09:20.826
It is a beautiful blade.

00:09:20.893 --> 00:09:23.529
But today, your opponent's was
just a little sharper, a little

00:09:23.595 --> 00:09:25.197
lighter, and faster to wield.

00:09:25.297 --> 00:09:27.132
 Justin, again, thank you
very much for your hard work.

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:28.567
But unfortunately,
I'm gonna have to ask

00:09:28.667 --> 00:09:29.869
you to please leave the forge.

00:09:29.969 --> 00:09:30.969
 Good job.

00:09:31.036 --> 00:09:32.505
 Thank you.

00:09:32.605 --> 00:09:36.275
 I'm glad that this time
frame worked out as it did.

00:09:36.341 --> 00:09:38.210
Chemo takes a toll on your body.

00:09:38.311 --> 00:09:40.379
But going through
that and then do

00:09:40.479 --> 00:09:44.616
this whole competition just
goes to show myself how much

00:09:44.716 --> 00:09:47.253
I can actually push through.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:48.587
I've always kind
of been stubborn.

00:09:48.654 --> 00:09:53.625
But I never realized how
stubborn I am, actually.

00:09:53.726 --> 00:09:57.129
 Well, Doug, you are the
"Forged in Fire" Champion.

00:09:57.229 --> 00:09:58.263
Congratulations, man.

00:09:58.364 --> 00:09:58.998
 Thanks a lot.

00:09:59.098 --> 00:10:00.132
[applause]

00:10:00.199 --> 00:10:01.633
 What are you gonna to
get with that $10,000?

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:04.136
 Oh, I fell in love
with a forge press.

00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:05.438
[laughs]

00:10:05.504 --> 00:10:07.573
I definitely want one
of those of my own.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:08.774
I'm ecstatic.

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:10.308
You know, I put a
lot of heart and soul

00:10:10.375 --> 00:10:11.676
into making that blade.

00:10:11.776 --> 00:10:13.479
And seeing it perform
the way that it did

00:10:13.545 --> 00:10:15.581
was just a really
great confirmation

00:10:15.681 --> 00:10:16.949
about my abilities.

00:10:17.016 --> 00:10:18.684
And it's just making me
want to make more knives.

00:10:18.751 --> 00:10:22.355
[heavy metal music]

